 extreme  it's an accurate portrayal of most  schools in the u.s. we tether our  children to desks tablets tables and  tests all day long and compounding the  issue is that fewer and fewer kids walk  or ride their bikes to school more often  they're writing to school in cars or  buses all of these factors work in  concert with one another  to reduce physical activity in addition  sedentism and screen time may have  additional negative consequences for  behavior attention span and physical  health in the past 30 years rates of  obesity and children have doubled and  quadrupled in adolescents according to  the Centers for Disease Control or CDC a  recent estimate on their website fact  sheet suggests that more than one-third  of kids and teenagers in the US are  currently overweight or obese in  addition to the same risk factor seen in  overweight and obese adults including  cardiovascular disease high blood  pressure and some types of cancer kids  and teenagers also exhibit higher risk  for type 2 diabetes and pre-diabetes  which is a condition where blood glucose  levels suggest a higher risk for the  development of diabetes they're also at  higher risk for things like sleep apnea  bone and joint problems and  psychological problems like poor  self-esteem the lack of physical  activity we just discussed is a big  factor in why our kids are becoming more  and more overweight and our lack of  physical activity appears to be at a  mismatch with the conditions in which we  evolved a recent study was undertaken by  a group of my colleagues who also work  with the hodza foragers of Tanzania the  study led by Dave Reichman an  anthropologist from the University of  Arizona looked at the time that the  hodza spent in moderate to vigorous  physical activity more time spent in  this type of physical activity is a  predictor for cardiovascular health  there's a notion that our current  physiology requires aerobic exercise  because we evolved as hunter-gatherers  and spent so much more time moving than  we do today  the u.s. office of disease prevention  and health promotion suggests that we  should be getting at least 150 minutes  of moderate to vigorous activity per  week  the research team wanted to figure out  how much the hodza get so they used  heart rate monitors to measure their  levels of exertion and how much the  hodza moved during a typical day what  they found is that the hodza spend large  amounts of time engaging in physical  activity about 135 minutes per day  which is almost as much as Americans are  encouraged to do per week these data are  important because they support the idea  that cardiovascular health  is connected with physical activity and  was likely a key part of the evolution  of our physiology this study also  underscores how little physical activity  we engage in here in the u.s. everything  we've just talked about indicates that  sedentism is contributing to higher  rates of obesity and heart disease but  it isn't only about not moving enough  it's also about diet composition we're  now eating three times the amount of  sugar that we ate 30 years ago according  to several estimates the average  American eats over 60 pounds of sugar a  year not including fruits and fruit  juices we all seem to have a love/hate  relationship with sugar we love the  taste of it but we hate its consequences  as we've talked about glucose is  something that we need during our  evolutionary history it's likely that a  shot of sugar like honey had positive  effects on things like the expansion of  our brains and this is because our  brains are glucose consumers they use it  as fuel that powers billions of neuronal  nerve cells when we ingest food glucose  is absorbed from our intestines into the  bloodstream and then distributed  throughout our bodies our neurons need  an almost constant supply of glucose  because they can't store the sugar  themselves the sweet tooth may not only  be part of our evolutionary heritage but  it's also a characteristic of  development research has suggested that  infants and children like sweet tastes  more than their adult counterparts and  interestingly our brains respond  differently to different types of sugar  this might be a good time for us to talk  about what carbohydrates are and what we  mean when we say sugar carbohydrates are  the sugars starches and fibers that are  found in foods sugars can either be  single sugars called monosaccharides  like glucose or fructose or they can be  double sugars called disaccharides which  includes sucrose or table sugar and  toasts the sugar and milk the  distinction between simple and complex  carbohydrates can often be confusing but  if we have a handle on the different  types of sugar it becomes less daunting  to sort out the differences simple  sugars are simple carbohydrates made of  one or two sugars they're found in all  sorts of foods like jams jellies honey  table sugar candy and fruit juices and  they break down fairly quickly meaning  that they're digested rapidly and can  act as a source of energy it's not only  processed foods however as things like  milk are also classified as containing a  simple sugar complex carbohydrates or  polysaccharides which means many sugars  and they're composed of starches made by  longer saccharide chains and this means  that complex carbs which includes foods  like peas beans corn oats rye and some  veggies take longer to break down  complex carbs provide prolonged energy  and they also stabilize blood sugar  rather than spiking it in the way that  simple carbohydrates do and our brains  handle different types of sugar  differently glucose can help suppress  the part of the brain that makes us want  to eat while fructose does the opposite  according to neurological experiments  done after eating both types fructose  amplifies the reward centers of our  brain making us want it more we get the  bulk of our fructose in foods that are  sweetened with high fructose corn syrup  and from sweetened drinks syrups and  dessert foods high fructose corn syrup  is absorbed quickly so it doesn't  stimulate the production of leptin or  insulin and this is the mechanism at  play that triggers the body's signals  for satiation which is what can lead to  overeating if you don't feel full and it  triggers reward centers it makes sense  that the more we consume the more we  want to consume the Department of  Agriculture estimates that the average  American consumes about 27 pounds of  high fructose corn syrup a year that  number might seem a bit shocking but  when you actually stop to look at how  many foods and drinks contain the  sweetener it becomes easier to  understand how we consume so much of it  let's  take soda for example some estimates  suggest that one in five Americans drink  at least one soda everyday and some  estimates like a recent Gallup poll  suggest that it could be far more that  half of Americans currently drink soda  on a daily basis they represent some of  the most consumed beverage --is at about  forty five gallons of soda consumed per  person per year despite these numbers  Americans are actually reducing their  soda consumption there's been a national  move towards reducing the intake of soda  and many of us will remember the 2013  sugary drinks portion cap rule in New  York City known colloquially as the soda  ban it moved to regulate the size of  sodas sold at restaurants delis stadiums  movie theaters and even food carts the  ban attempted to ban the selling of  sugary drinks and cups larger than about  16 ounces after several years locked up  in the courts the state's highest court  decided that the ban was an overstep of  the New York City Board of Health and  the band never went through it's really  a fascinating history to track how our  sodas got so big in the first place when  the coca-cola company teamed up with  McDonald's in 1955 their fountain soda  was a seven ounce cup forty years later  a soda offered at McDonald's was  approximately six times larger and this  isn't even close to the largest size  available many convenience stores offer  soda cups that hold 85 ounces of soda  which is at least twice the amount that  the average stomach can hold but it  looks like the u.s. soda habit is waning  it's currently at the lowest level in 30  years and the trend is continuing  downward the attempted New York soda ban  is only one example many companies have  reduced their 64 ounce soda cups not as  a reaction against the sugar or calorie  content but because they don't fit  easily into car cupholders the first  trend away from soda sweetened with high  fructose corn syrup was a move towards  diet sodas sweetened with artificial  sweeteners like saccharine  aspartame and sucralose well originally  thought to be better as they reduced the  calorie intake of the beverage we now  know that they're not the miracle  sweetener we originally thought they  were it turns out that these sugar  substitutes have the same effect on our  bodies as sugar does they trigger  insulin and send our body into fat  storage mode which tends to lead to  weight gain the other sugary drinks that  people turn to in lieu of soda are  energy drinks and iced teas and most  iced teas while seemingly a healthy  alternative can contain 60 grams of  sugar and 250 calories energy drinks are  also not a healthy option many of the  most popular brands have as many  calories and grams of sugar as soda or  more coffee drinks can also contain a  surprising amount of calories and sugar  and some coffee drinks even contain more  calories than a fast-food burger which  isn't something that most of us think  about another contributing factor beyond  calorie rich foods and sweet beverages  is portion size the supersize phenomenon  has been written about extensively in  nutrition research as well as in the  popular media portion sizes have changed  drastically over the last several  decades  the average American eats about 25% more  today than they did in the early 1970s  and it's not just portion sizes it's  plate sizes as well nutritionists are  urging people to attempt to shift their  portions and their plates back to the  size that they were in the 1950s a  recent article in the UK news outlet  Daily Mail reported that the simple act  of eating off of a smaller plate might  reduce caloric intake by almost 160  calories each meal other recommendations  to combat large portions include a  larger plate for salad as long as your  main course is on a small plate other  tips include not placing food at the  table during meals as it encourages  second helpings refraining from watching  TV while you eat and limit eating out to  about once a week when you do eat out at  a restaurant nutrition experts urge  people to order veggie  and protein avoiding carbs as much as  possible and suggest getting it to go  box at the beginning of a meal and then  taking half of the meal home as the Mayo  Clinic website points out people tend to  eat more when offered more so if we get  a take-home box at the outset of the  meal and pack up half of our food we may  be less likely to overindulge the Mayo  Clinic also provides some handy tricks  for people who are unsure what proper  serving sizes might be not the serving  sizes listed on the back of food  packaging as those are based on the  amount of food people tend to actually  eat not the amount of food that they're  recommended to eat to help combat this  confusing food labeling and portion  sizing conundrum the Mayo Clinic  suggests memorizing the average size of  some everyday items to avoid to aid in  estimating portion size for example one  vegetable serving is about the size of a  baseball one fruit serving should be  about the size of a tennis ball or an  apple whereas foods like pasta or bread  should be limited to the size of a  hockey puck the recommended serving size  for cheese is equivalent to about four  dice and fat like butter about two dice  a serving of meat should be about the  same size as a deck of cards most people  are not aware that we're experiencing  this phenomenon of portion Distortion as  they focus on the serving sizes that are  listed on packaging it seems like the  true enemy and the global war on obesity  changes often from fat to sugar to  additives for example making it hard to  know precisely what might be to blame  for the pandemic that we're experiencing  now it's likely a combination of changes  in diet shifting towards processed fatty  energy dense foods in a world where  physical activity levels are declining  rapidly  we're producing a lot consuming a lot  and wasting a lot the United Nations  Environment Program recently estimated  that about a third of all edible food  produced is wasted this is equivalent to  about  trillion dollars or about one out of  every four calories that we eat as  people are becoming ever more conscious  about their weight and cardio metabolic  health they're also looking for ways to  reduce food waste the UN suggests that  people start the process with being  mindful about the types of food that  they're eating and encourages people to  save to save their money save their food  and by doing so save the environment the  global prevalence of over nutrition is  incredibly high some estimates suggest  that nearly two billion people out of  the nearly eight billion on the planet  are very overweight or obese more people  are now dying from diseases associated  with over nutrition and under nutrition  that being said over 1 billion people in  the world are also dying from  malnutrition given this nutrition gap  many researchers and public policymakers  don't think that the issue is the  availability of resources but it's the  allocation of those resources many  middle and low income countries around  the world now face what the World Health  Organization calls a double burden of  disease they simultaneously contend with  existing problems of infectious disease  and under nutrition as well as obesity  the numbers suggest that it's possible  to find over nutrition and under  nutrition existing in the same community  and even the same household and it's  this puzzle that will return to next  time  you

